---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const LAST_UPDATED = '2026-11-24';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Disclosure"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Disclosure', path: '/disclosure/' },
  ]}
  description="The independence and monetisation policy of singletenant.ai: what money can never buy, how any future ads and sponsorship would be labelled, and the commercial relationships that exist today."
>
  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 md:py-16">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Legal</p>
      <h1 class="m-0 mb-2" style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Disclosure
      </h1>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs mb-10" style="color: var(--color-mute);">
        Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}
      </p>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This site is independent. Today it earns nothing: it carries no
          advertising, no sponsorship and no referral links. It may carry
          labelled advertising and, later, sponsorship, but only under the
          rules on this page, which are published before any of them exists.
          This page also lists every commercial relationship that does exist.
        </p>

        <h2 id="independence">What money never affects</h2>
        <p>
          Advertising, sponsorship, and referral commission never influence:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>the vendor matrix, or any fact recorded in it;</li>
          <li>the ordering of vendors, which is alphabetical;</li>
          <li>the comparison pages, which are generated from the same data;</li>
          <li>
            which vendors are included, which follows the published
            <a href="/methodology/">inclusion criteria</a> only;
          </li>
          <li>
            the wording of any profile, comparison, guide or analysis.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          No one can buy placement, ranking, inclusion, a better description
          or a "verified" status, and no one can pay to have a correction
          made or withheld.
        </p>
        <p>
          Vendor facts are verified against primary sources, and every
          populated fact links to its evidence with the date it was checked. A
          fact that cannot be verified is shown as "Not verified" whatever the
          vendor's commercial relationship with this site. Advertisers and
          sponsors get no preview of, or say over, editorial content. The
          <a href="/editorial-policy/#conflicts">editorial policy</a> covers
          conflicts of interest in full.
        </p>

        <h2 id="stages">When money comes in</h2>
        <p>
          Credibility comes first, so income is introduced in stages, each
          only once the one before has been earned:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Now: none.</strong> No ads, no sponsorship, no referral
            links, and no tracking: the site sets no cookies and runs no
            analytics script (see the <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Display advertising</strong>, once readership is large
            enough for an ad network to accept the site. The preference is
            contextual ads chosen by page topic, not by tracking readers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sponsorship</strong>, only once the site has a record of
            steady readership, citations and working corrections, under
            published terms consistent with this page.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Each stage starts only with a reviewed change to the site, and this
          page and the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> are updated when it
          does.
        </p>

        <h2 id="referral-links">Referral links</h2>
        <p>
          The site carries no referral or affiliate links. If it ever does,
          each one will be labelled "Ad" in the link itself, with a notice
          that the site may earn commission, and will carry the
          <code>rel="sponsored"</code> attribute. A vendor with a referral
          link will be held to exactly the same standard as one without.
        </p>

        <h2 id="advertising">Advertising</h2>
        <p>
          If the site carries advertising, each ad sits in a clearly marked
          slot labelled "Advertisement", separate from the editorial content,
          with at most one ad per page. Ads never appear inside the vendor
          matrix, a comparison table, or a vendor's facts, and buying an ad
          buys nothing else.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sponsorship">Sponsored content</h2>
        <p>
          Paid-for content, if there is ever any, is labelled
          "Advertisement feature" at the top, with the advertiser named, and
          links to this page. The label is never just "Sponsored": the UK
          advertising regulator advises against "Sponsored" on its own because
          it doesn't make clear that the advertiser paid for the content.
          Paid-for content sits apart from the vendor matrix, the comparisons
          and the data, and is never presented as independent analysis.
          Sponsors and advertisers never see or approve editorial content
          before it is published, and their links carry
          <code>rel="sponsored"</code>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="relationships">Current relationships</h2>
        <p>
          The editor, Simon Newton, holds a referral account with
          <strong>RunPod</strong>. The site carries no RunPod referral link.
          The site has no advertisers or sponsors, and no other vendor in the
          database has a commercial relationship with this site or its editor. This list is
          updated whenever a relationship starts or ends, and the change is
          recorded in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
        <p>
          Identifying paid-for content and links is required by the UK CAP
          Code (enforced by the ASA) and by Part 4 of the Digital Markets,
          Competition and Consumers Act 2024 (enforced by the CMA); the labels
          above follow the ASA, CAP and CMA
          <a href="https://www.asa.org.uk/resource/influencers-guide.html">guide to making clear that ads are ads</a>
          (2026 edition). For US readers, the FTC's endorsement guides
          (16 CFR Part 255) apply. Questions about this policy:
          <a href="mailto:simon@singletenant.ai">simon@singletenant.ai</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
